<doc><paragraph heading='1'><text>L2. &quot;Veganism is a cult, a religion&quot;</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>Thesis:</bold></text><text> labeling a position a &quot;cult&quot; is a way of not assessing it. Veganism rests on public arguments that anyone can examine: animals feel, we can live without killing them, and causing unnecessary suffering is wrong. A religion asks for faith; veganism asks us to look at the facts and at the consistency of our own values.</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>Family:</bold></text><text> L. Attacks on veganism and vegans · </text><text><bold>Part:</bold></text><text> IND</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='2'><text>1. Formulation</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>Everyday version:</bold></text><text> &quot;You preach like fanatics. That&#x27;s indoctrination.&quot;</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>Strongest version (steelman):</bold></text></paragraph><list kind='ordered'><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>P1.</bold></text><text> Some vegans speak of veganism with fervor, rituals and group identity similar to religious ones.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>P2.</bold></text><text> Religious beliefs can&#x27;t be imposed on those who don&#x27;t share them.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>P3.</bold></text><text> Veganism is a belief of that kind.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>C.</bold></text><text> So vegans shouldn&#x27;t try to persuade others.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph><text>P1 may be true in some cases. The problem lies in P3: veganism doesn&#x27;t depend on faith, but on facts and arguments everyone can assess.</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>The argument is </text><text><bold>valid</bold></text><text>. Its </text><text><bold>soundness</bold></text><text> fails at P3.</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='2'><text>2. How it can show up</text></paragraph><table><row><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Context</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Typical form</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Everyday conversation</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>&quot;You preach like fanatics.&quot;</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Everyday conversation</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>&quot;That&#x27;s indoctrination.&quot;</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Everyday conversation</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>&quot;Veganism is brainwashing.&quot;</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Everyday conversation</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>&quot;Veganism is a religion.&quot;</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Social media</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>&quot;The vegan cult wants to convert everyone.&quot;</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Academic version</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>&quot;Veganism functions as a secular religion.&quot;</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Disguised version</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>&quot;Veganism is a political ideology or a globalist agenda.&quot; (which is J7)</text></paragraph></cell></row></table><paragraph heading='2'><text>3. Answer for children</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>A religion asks people to believe in things you can&#x27;t see. Veganism is different: it talks about things you can check, like &quot;animals feel pain&quot; and &quot;you can live well without eating meat&quot;. Anyone can think about this and reach their own conclusion.</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='2'><text>4. Short answer</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>Calling it a cult is a way of not discussing it. A religion asks for faith; veganism rests on claims that can be checked: animals feel, well-planned vegan diets are adequate, and causing unnecessary suffering is wrong, which almost everyone already accepts. Defending a position with conviction doesn&#x27;t make it religious. If it did, any cause defended with passion would be a cult.</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>Question to ask back:</bold></text><text> &quot;Which part of the argument depends on faith rather than facts and reasons?&quot;</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='2'><text>5. Full answer</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='3'><text>(a) Reconstruction and diagnosis</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>The argument labels instead of assessing:</text></paragraph><table><row><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Feature of a religion</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Does veganism have it?</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Belief in entities or revealed truths</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>No</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Sacred authority</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>No</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Verifiable claims</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Yes: animal sentience, nutritional adequacy</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>A widely accepted moral principle</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Yes: don&#x27;t cause unnecessary suffering</text></paragraph></cell></row></table><paragraph heading='3'><text>(b) Where the argument breaks</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>1. Labeling isn&#x27;t refuting.</bold></text><text> Calling a position a cult doesn&#x27;t show it&#x27;s wrong.</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>2. Veganism rests on public arguments.</bold></text><text> Animal sentience (A5), nutritional adequacy (G1) and the principle of not causing unnecessary suffering can be assessed by anyone.</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>3. Conviction isn&#x27;t fanaticism.</bold></text><text> Firmly defending a moral position is common in many causes.</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>4. Eating meat also involves beliefs.</bold></text><text> They&#x27;re simply invisible because they&#x27;re dominant (E9).</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>5. Vegans are diverse.</bold></text><text> There are religious vegans and atheist vegans, from many cultures and political positions.</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='3'><text>(c) Data and sources</text></paragraph><table><row><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Source</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>What it says</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Note</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text><link href="https://sites.google.com/nyu.edu/nydeclaration/declaration">The New York Declaration on Animal Consciousness (2024)</link></text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Strong scientific support for consciousness in mammals and birds</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Shows the basis of veganism is verifiable</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text><link href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27886704/">Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics, 2016</link></text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Well-planned vegan diets are adequate</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>An empirical basis, not a basis in faith</text></paragraph></cell></row></table><paragraph heading='3'><text>(d) The strongest counter-reply: the group behavior is religious</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>The most serious version grants that veganism&#x27;s reasons are public, but says the social behavior of many vegans resembles that of religious groups: strong identity, their own language, exclusion of those who don&#x27;t follow.</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='3'><text>(e) Why the counter-reply doesn&#x27;t reach the conclusion</text></paragraph><list kind='ordered'><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>Every group has social dynamics.</bold></text><text> Sports fans, parties and professions do too.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>Group behavior doesn&#x27;t change the reasons.</bold></text><text> The question is still about the animals.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>It&#x27;s a collective ad hominem.</bold></text><text> It judges the idea by its followers.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph heading='3'><text>(f) Back to the moral question</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>The cult label avoids the question. But the question doesn&#x27;t require faith: animals feel, and we can live without killing them. What justifies carrying on?</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='2'><text>6. Connections</text></paragraph><table><row><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Direction</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Code</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Why it appears</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Comes before</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>A19 &quot;Vegans anthropomorphize animals&quot;</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Treats concern as fanaticism</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Comes before</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>E9 &quot;Eating meat isn&#x27;t an ideology; veganism is&quot;</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Veganism becomes a cult</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Comes before</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>J7 &quot;Veganism is a political ideology or a globalist agenda&quot;</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Veganism is a cult</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Comes before</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>L1 &quot;Vegans are annoying and think they are better&quot;</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Annoyance becomes fanaticism</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Comes before</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>L3 &quot;Hitler was a vegetarian&quot;</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Veganism is a cult</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Comes after</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>L11 &quot;Vegan activists are radicals and extremists&quot;</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Fanaticism becomes extremism</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Comes after</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>E2 &quot;Morality is relative&quot;</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>If it&#x27;s a belief, everyone would have their own</text></paragraph></cell></row></table><paragraph heading='2'><text>7. Reasoning errors</text></paragraph><table><row><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Level</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Error</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>How it appears in L2</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Logical</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Labeling</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Classifies in order not to assess</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Logical</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>False analogy</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Compares moral conviction with religious faith</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Epistemological</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Ignoring the empirical basis</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Disregards the facts veganism rests on</text></paragraph></cell></row></table><paragraph heading='2'><text>8. Contradictions</text></paragraph><list kind='ordered'><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>Has strong moral convictions.</bold></text><text> And doesn&#x27;t consider them a religion.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>Champions other causes with passion.</bold></text><text> Without thinking it&#x27;s fanaticism.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>Follows cultural food norms.</bold></text><text> Which are also shared beliefs.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph heading='2'><text>9. Biases</text></paragraph><table><row><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Bias</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>How it sustains L2</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Evidence</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Do-gooder derogation</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Discrediting those who act morally eases discomfort</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text><link href="https://faculty.wharton.upenn.edu/wp-content/uploads/2011/12/Minson---Monin,-2011.pdf">Minson and Monin, 2012</link></text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Group identity</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Veganism is seen as &quot;the other group&quot;</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>General literature on identity-protective cognition</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Naive realism and the bias blind spot</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Those who disagree seem fanatical rather than people with other reasons</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text><link href="https://questaodeetica.biblioverse.org/en/v10/04-cognitive-biases-and-their-influence-on-our-decisions.html">Questão de Ética, vol. X, ch. 4</link></text></paragraph></cell></row></table><paragraph heading='2'><text>10. Extrapolations</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>The principle being tested is: &quot;if a position is defended with conviction, it&#x27;s religious&quot;.</text></paragraph><table><row><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Applied to</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>What would follow</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Note</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>The anti-smoking movement</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>It would be a cult</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Absurd</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Defending human rights</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>It would be a religion</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Absurd</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Veganism</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>It would be a cult</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>The same error</text></paragraph></cell></row></table><paragraph heading='2'><text>11. What the argument gets right</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>It&#x27;s true that some vegans speak with fervor and that groups with a strong identity can become closed. It&#x27;s also true that no one likes to feel indoctrinated, and that respectful communication works better.</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>But veganism doesn&#x27;t ask for faith. It asks us to look at facts and at the consistency of our own values, something anyone can do.</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='2'><text>References</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>Science</bold></text></paragraph><list kind='bullet'><listItem><paragraph><text><link href="https://sites.google.com/nyu.edu/nydeclaration/declaration">The New York Declaration on Animal Consciousness (2024)</link></text><text>.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem><paragraph><text><link href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27886704/">Melina, V., Craig, W., &amp; Levin, S. (2016). Position of the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics: Vegetarian Diets. *Journal of the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics*, 116(12)</link></text><text>.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph><text><bold>Psychology</bold></text></paragraph><list kind='bullet'><listItem><paragraph><text><link href="https://faculty.wharton.upenn.edu/wp-content/uploads/2011/12/Minson---Monin,-2011.pdf">Minson, J. A., &amp; Monin, B. (2012). Do-gooder derogation. *Social Psychological and Personality Science*, 3(2)</link></text><text>.</text></paragraph></listItem></list></doc>